#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228783 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6329ab32-968f-5041-b2e3-12dde408968a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Implicit Euler Method"}] :question/id #uuid "185f4059-c0d2-59a0-932b-d1152c407db6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21317" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = y^3 - x\\,y, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x = 1$, the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n1. $g(y_\\text{new}) = (y_\\text{new})^3 - 3y_\\text{new} - 1$\n2. $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + 1$\n3. $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 - 1$\n4. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new}$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new} \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new} \\\\\ny_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} + x_{new}y_{new} - y_(⏟)_(g(y_{new})) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new} - y\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} + 2y_{new} - 1 \\\\\n&= 3y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228784 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866481fb-c460-5a39-a7a0-84b64d8bd673" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Implicit Euler Method: One Step"}] :question/id #uuid "2e45e65d-ffd1-5418-9445-eee71cb36ded" :question/math-academy-id "e-21319" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2xy + y^3, \\qquad y(1.5)=-0.3$\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2)$. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1.5 = 0.5\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (2x_{new}y_{new} + (y_{new})^{3}) \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= x_{new}y_{new} + 0.5(y_{new})^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + x_{new}y_{new} + 0.5(y_{new})^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 0.5\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1.5 + 0.5 = 2\n$$\n\nThe old $y$ value is $y = -0.3$. Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= 0.5(y_{new})^{3} + (2 - 1)y_{new} - 0.3 \\\\\n&= 0.5(y_{new})^{3} + y_{new} - 0.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-0.3$ to solve\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0\n$$\n\nWe'll use Newton's method.\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 1.5(y_\\text{new})^2 + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{0.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^3 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 0.3}{1.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + 1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with\n\n$$\ny(1.5) = -0.3\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y_{new})_{1} &= -0.3 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx 0.240529 \\\\ (y_{new})_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 0.288849 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{4} \\mid \\approx 0.288050\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx 0.29\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6329ab32-968f-5041-b2e3-12dde408968a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Function on Which to Apply a Root-Finding Method When Applying the Implicit Euler Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecc8ae4f-d092-5277-810a-d3cb91b6c6c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = (y_{new})^{3} - y_{new} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227522} {:answer/id #uuid "0428d799-00aa-53a8-8033-0690ca656130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227523} {:answer/id #uuid "4ed86dcc-af4a-5291-a93f-87409fdedcaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} + x_{new}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227524} {:answer/id #uuid "5a88e782-8974-56b5-bc1b-b86dbdf5c000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = (y_{new})^{3} - y_{new} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227525} {:answer/id #uuid "73c61869-f4a5-577f-8f65-6dc509cbb513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73c61869-f4a5-577f-8f65-6dc509cbb513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "g(y_{new}) = y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227526} :answer-field/id #uuid "28e36cb8-8670-5c61-8e5c-c6fa9576318e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227521}] :question/id #uuid "106b38b1-7560-51f1-b255-f587828e56e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-325128" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = y^{3} - x, y(1) = 1$\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx = 1$, the new value $y_{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_{new})$ used in the first step?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866481fb-c460-5a39-a7a0-84b64d8bd673" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using the Implicit Euler Method: One Step"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "d42eb830-acd8-5822-a929-9a9d6b943d81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186227527}] :question/id #uuid "7bfc747b-4f72-569b-afba-2705d78bce41" :question/math-academy-id "q-325137" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x^{2} - y^{3}, y(0.5) = 1$\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1)$. Round your answer to two decimal places.$y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
